    id: "lighting-layers",
    title: "Lighting Layers",
    topics: ["light", "lighting", "lamp", "pendant", "dim"],
    text: "# Lighting Layers\n\n## Three layers every room needs\n\n1. Ambient \u2014 overall glow (ceiling flush, pendant, recessed on dimmers, or reflected uplight).\n2. Task \u2014 reading, cooking, desk, makeup (floor lamp, under-cabinet, swing-arm, vanity).\n3. Accent \u2014 art wash, shelf light, candle-level glow that adds depth at night.\n\nA single bright ceiling fixture is not a lighting plan. Layers let you shift from cleaning mode to evening mode without changing the furniture.\n\n## Practical rules\n\n- Prefer warm white (2700\u20133000K) in living and bedrooms; cooler task light only where detail work needs it.\n- Put ambient and key lamps on dimmers whenever possible.\n- Height: pendants over tables leave clearance for heads and sightlines; dining pendants typically hang so the bottom sits roughly 30\u201336 inches above the table (adjust for ceiling height).\n- Floor lamps work beside sofas when tables are scarce; avoid blocking walkways.\n- Avoid only downlights; bounce light off walls/ceilings for softness.\n\n## Style cues\n\n- MCM: sculptural floor or sputnik + soft ambient.\n- Scandi/Japandi: paper, linen, or frosted glass; gentle pools of light.\n- Industrial: cage/track plus warm dimming \u2014 not harsh warehouse glare.\n- Traditional: chandelier or lantern ambient + paired table lamps.\n- Coastal: simple pendants, linen shades, daylight first.\n\n## Do / Don't\n\nDo light dark corners and pathways. Do match lamp scale to furniture. Don't rely on overhead alone for living rooms. Don't mix five unrelated fixture styles; pick a family and vary size.\n\n## Room-by-room minimums\n\nLiving: ambient + two lamps + optional accent. Dining: dimmable pendant + sideboard lamp or wall lights. Bedroom: ambient soft + two bedside tasks + optional closet/task. Home office: ambient + desk task that does not bounce glare on screens. Kitchen: ambient + under-cabinet task + pendant over island if present.\n\n## Glare, shadows, and safety\n\nAvoid bare bulbs at eye level in conversation zones. Light stairs and hall changes of level. In kids' rooms, prioritize covered fixtures and warm enough light for wind-down. If recessed cans are the only option, add lamps anyway \u2014 cans alone flatten faces and furniture.\n\n## Bulbs and control\n\nMatch color temperature within a room. Use dimmers on ambient circuits. Smart bulbs help only if the user will actually set scenes; otherwise a dimmer switch is more reliable. Measure brightness by activity, not by maximum wattage bragging.\n\n## EXPLAIN anchors\n\nAmbient/task/accent triad, 2700\u20133000K living spaces, dimmers, pendant clearance over tables, do not rely on overhead alone, style-specific fixture families.\n\n## Field note\n\nRe-check the room after each major purchase: does the latest piece reinforce the documented direction, or does it reopen a style conflict? If it reopens conflict, name the tension and re-assert dominant versus accent before buying further. Prefer one decisive correction over three small compromises that blur the plan. Keep shopping lists short: core pieces first, accents last, and only after the readiness of the room plan justifies them.\n\n## Field note\n\nRe-check the room after each major purchase: does the latest piece reinforce the documented direction, or does it reopen a style conflict? If it reopens conflict, name the tension and re-assert dominant versus accent before buying further. Prefer one decisive correction over three small compromises that blur the plan. Keep shopping lists short: core pieces first, accents last, and only after the readiness of the room plan justifies them.\n\n## Field note\n\nRe-check the room after each major purchase: does the latest piece reinforce the documented direction, or does it reopen a style conflict? If it reopens conflict, name the tension and re-assert dominant versus accent before buying further. Prefer one decisive correction over three small compromises that blur the plan. Keep shopping lists short: core pieces first, accents last, and only after the readiness of the room plan justifies them.",
